---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import "../styles/focus.css";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { href } from "../lib/links";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A time scale on black: ninety-six gold tick marks that lengthen steadily from left to right, then a final pale tick left conspicuously short"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    Every upload bar you have ever watched was lying to you, and most of them
    were lying for good reasons.
  </p>

  <p>
    The system does not know how long this will take, and it has to say
    something anyway. This course spends a semester on that one sentence.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not a course about UX. It is a course about the twelve inches of
    screen between a user pressing <strong>Upload</strong> and finding out
    whether it worked.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>

  <p>
    The whole semester is one artefact. In week 1 you inherit a file-upload
    interface that sits at 99% for eleven seconds and then fails silently. By
    week 12 it tells the truth &mdash; including when the truth is
    <em>I don&rsquo;t know</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything in between is you finding out what your interface actually has
    evidence for: bytes, stages, elapsed time, an estimate, or nothing at all.
    You will build a state model and discover it was wrong. You will write three
    time estimators and measure how much each one lies to itself. You will turn
    the animation off and find out whether it was carrying information. You will
    watch three people use the thing and resist the urge to change everything
    they touched.
  </p>

  <p>
    No server is involved. Every transfer, stall, timeout and dropped connection
    is simulated locally, so you can reproduce any failure on demand. That is
    the thing you can never do with a real network, and it is the reason this
    subject can be taught at all.
  </p>

  <h2>The bar says 99%. What is left?</h2>

  <p>
    That is the whole course, and it has an answer. Three different things are
    usually being confused, and an interface that keeps them apart stops needing
    to sit at 99% at all.
  </p>

  <dl class="three-things">
    <dt>The percentage</dt>
    <dd>
      A fraction, and a fraction needs a <strong>denominator</strong>. Most bars
      count bytes handed to the network. On a fast connection that finishes
      early, and everything after it &mdash; hashing, checking, storing &mdash;
      is outside the number.
    </dd>

    <dt>The stages</dt>
    <dd>
      What the process is actually doing: read, hash, transfer, verify. A bar
      counting bytes is blind to three of those four. A bar counting stages sees
      all of them and moves in lurches, because the stages are not the same
      size.
    </dd>

    <dt>The completion condition</dt>
    <dd>
      The sentence that says when the interface may claim <em>done</em>. &ldquo;The
      last byte left this machine&rdquo;, &ldquo;the service acknowledged it&rdquo; and
      &ldquo;it would survive a power cut&rdquo; are three different sentences, seconds
      apart, and only the last one is what the user came for.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <p>
    <strong>A bar stuck at 99% is usually a denominator that ran out.</strong>
    The transfer it was counting has finished; the work the user is waiting for
    has not; and the interface has no vocabulary for the difference. Twelve
    weeks on that.
  </p>

  <h2>See it happen</h2>

  <p>
    The <a href={href("/simulator/")}>progress simulator</a> runs the five
    behaviours this course works through &mdash; a steady upload, stages at
    different speeds, an upload with no known total, a stall that recovers, and
    a failure you can retry. Everything in it is simulated in your browser; no
    file is read and no request is made.
  </p>

  <p>
    The one worth a minute is <strong>&ldquo;Stall, then recover&rdquo;</strong>.
    Here is what it does, so the page is worth reading even if you never press
    Start:
  </p>

  <ol class="walkthrough">
    <li>
      The bar advances normally to <strong>45%</strong> &mdash; 45 of the 100
      work units the four stages are worth.
    </li>
    <li>
      Then the transfer stops reporting. <strong>The bar freezes and stops
      animating entirely</strong>, because a moving bar would be claiming
      something is happening when nothing is.
    </li>
    <li>
      The elapsed counter keeps running. That, and not an error, is what tells
      you something is wrong &mdash; a counter reading 0:45 on a job that
      normally takes 0:06 has communicated a problem with no error handling at
      all.
    </li>
    <li>
      Eight seconds later the transfer resumes on its own and the bar picks up
      from 45%.
    </li>
    <li>
      It reaches <strong>100% only when a <code>committed</code> event
      arrives</strong> &mdash; not when the bytes finish. The page prints that
      condition next to the bar, which is the standard every student prototype
      is held to.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    There is a ×4 speed control. A site about waiting should not make you do any.
  </p>

  <h2>How it runs</h2>

  <p>
    Twelve weekly studios, three hours each, and <strong>four</strong> lectures.
    The lectures are set pieces: each one introduces something you could not
    work out at the machine, and the studio two days later applies it. The other
    eight weeks open with a short framing and then get out of the way.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <p>
    Second-year students who can write HTML and CSS and enough JavaScript to
    write a function that takes a callback. You will not need a background in
    psychology, and you are not permitted to cite one you do not have.
  </p>

  <h2>What this course is not</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a general interface design course.</strong> One widget, one
      interaction, twelve weeks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a psychology course.</strong> We read what the published work
      on perceived duration claims, and then we fail to replicate it in a room
      of twenty classmates, on purpose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not a course about making users feel better.</strong> Sometimes an
      honest interface is the more irritating one. That trade is the subject.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>

  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The twelve weeks, each with the question it answers.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>All four of them, and what each one carries.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Progress simulator" href="/simulator/">
      <p>The five behaviours of a waiting interface, in one page.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Three pieces, 20/30/50, none of them a final exam.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches this and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .three-things {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem 1.25rem;
    margin-block: 1rem 1.75rem;
  }

  .three-things dt {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .three-things dd {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }

  .walkthrough {
    margin-block: 1rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .walkthrough li {
    margin-block-end: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
</style>
